From 114678f273b2e3f0afba8ee5706fc4248a84a87f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stepan Mazurov <smazurov@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:18:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] uboot-airoha: align the Gemtek W1700K chainloader with
 the XR1710G

The XR1710G chainloader was derived from the W1700K one and fixed on
the way. Apply the same changes to the W1700K, so both boards boot,
recover and install the same way:

- CONFIG_PINCONF is replaced by CONFIG_PINCTRL_AIROHA_AN7581. Without
  the pin controller driver the W1700K finds neither the reset button
  nor the status LEDs ("LED 'status:red' not found (err=-19)").
- bootcmd was "boot_ubi" without "run", and the default menu entry ran
  boot_production only, so a broken production volume never fell back
  to the recovery volume. Both now run boot_ubi, which falls back to
  the recovery volume and then to TFTP until a server answers.
  boot_recovery only runs bootm after the recovery volume was read.
- Holding the reset button when the chainloader starts loads the
  recovery image via TFTP. It has to be pressed after power is applied:
  held at power-on, the vendor bootloader stops at "Press x".
- A ubi partition that does not attach is formatted, missing ubootenv,
  ubootenv2 and factory volumes are created, and the environment is
  saved once, so fw_printenv and fw_setenv work. CONFIG_ENV_SIZE is the
  size uboot-envtools uses, and MMC support is disabled: the board has
  no eMMC, and the first saveenv went there.
- The TFTP server is 192.168.1.254, as for other OpenWrt U-Boot boards.
- The installer variable and menu entry are dropped: the factory volume
  is now built by sysupgrade, so the device is installed by booting the
  recovery image and running sysupgrade.
- CONFIG_CMD_ASKENV is enabled, so failed menu entries wait for Enter.

The patch is renamed to the 4xx range used by the other boards.

Installation (via serial console):

1. Connect to the UART header and interrupt the vendor bootloader.
2. Make the vendor bootloader boot the chainloader from flash, write
   the chainloader, and erase the rest of the stock firmware, which
   keeps its own UBI in this area:

     setenv serverip 192.168.1.254
     setenv ipaddr 192.168.1.1
     tftpboot 0x89000000 openwrt-airoha-an7581-gemtek_w1700k-ubi-chainload-uboot.itb
     setenv one flash read 0x600000 0x100000 \$loadaddr
     setenv two "; bootm"
     setenv bootcmd "$one$two"
     saveenv
     flash erase 0x600000 0x100000
     flash write 0x600000 0x100000 0x89000000
     flash erase 0x700000 0x1b700000
     reset

   The vendor Ethernet driver is flaky; tftpboot may need a few
   attempts, or use "loady 0x89000000" and YMODEM instead.
3. The chainloader creates UBI with the ubootenv, ubootenv2 and an
   empty factory volume, saves its environment and, with nothing to
   boot, loads openwrt-airoha-an7581-gemtek_w1700k-ubi-initramfs-recovery.itb
   via TFTP from 192.168.1.254.
4. In the recovery system (192.168.1.1), run sysupgrade with the
   sysupgrade.itb image. It fills the factory volume from the vendor
   partition and installs OpenWrt.

Devices installed with the UBI installer keep their volumes; the new
chainloader only saves its environment on the first boot.

Signed-off-by: Stepan Mazurov <smazurov@gmail.com>
